From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: CPU OPPs -- the vendor's voltage per speed bin

What. Every CPU operating point gets one opp-microvolt-speed<b> column
per speed bin that runs it: speed1/2/3/6 (fuse 0x01/0x02/0x03/0x06 ->
vf0100/0200/0300/0600) and speed20/21 (0x14/0x15 -> vf0401/vf0501), next
to the measured speed4/speed5. opp-supported-hw, opp-hz and the unnamed
opp-microvolt do not change.

Why. arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opps-per-bin-frequency-sets took the vendor's
frequency set per bin but ran every bin other than 4 and 5 at the
unnamed worst case, the maximum over all columns. That is up to 210 mV
above what the vendor ships the same die with (fuse 0x15 at A55 1612
MHz: 840 mV against 1050 mV), and dynamic power grows with V^2 -- a die
of such a bin ran hotter and throttled earlier than on the vendor's
kernel, for a margin nothing supports. The worst case is no better
evidence than the columns: it is their maximum.

Source. The per-bin voltages exist only in the vendor's
sun60iw2p1-cpu-vf.dtsi (Allwinner BSP cubie-aiot-v1.5.0, sha256
d5535630db3d0acdeace832d1dfad684fab9ab0c31c482b493e36a7f3387e5ad; the
CPU columns are unchanged since cubie-aiot-v1.4.8), 26 MHz family: the
UM V1.00 and the datasheet V1.01 have no per-bin table, only VDD-CPUB
and VDD-CPUL 0.8..1.05 V (table 5-2), and every column stays inside
that. The vendor ships every die of a bin with its column.
The columns and the masks are generated by a script whose
--check mode verifies both against the vendor file.

Where the vendor driver differs, and what this patch does:
  - the same as for the frequency sets: fuse 0x00 (vf0000) and
    0x11/0x12 are not carried, so those dies take the vf0100 fallback.
  - speed4 and speed5 are ours, measured at the PMIC on the 6 GiB and
    the 8 GiB board; the script checks that they equal vf0400/vf0500 at
    every vendor point and never rewrites them.
  - 247 and 494 MHz are ours; every bin that runs them gets the 800 mV
    those points request, which each bin already runs at 416 and
    1014 MHz.
  - the unnamed worst case stays as a safety net: the OPP core falls
    back to it if a named column is ever missing.

The fallback. vf0100 equals the worst case at every point bin 1 runs,
so a die the driver cannot identify (index 1) runs the same voltages as
before this patch -- now also exactly what the vendor gives an
unmatched fuse value.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 after the full series; the dtb builds with
W=1 and no warning (6.18.54, native arm64); in the dtb, A55 1404 MHz
speed3 = 860 mV (vf0300), A76 2002 MHz speed5 = 1000 mV, speed21 =
960 mV, and speed1/speed3 absent there because those bins do not run
2002 MHz; --check passes. Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-29, with a
test-only fuse-word override (not part of this series): the rail at every
point equals the column of bins 1, 2, 3, 6 and 0x14. Fuse 0x15 is not run there: vf0501 lies up to 40 mV
under bin 5 at the top points, which would undervolt that die.

Assisted-by: Claude
